{:edb/db-before-t 1446 :edb/db-after-t 1447 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534759 50 #inst "2026-10-12T10:57:54.353Z" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186140333 1075 #edb/ref 17592186435201 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186140333 1078 #edb/ref 17592186435201 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142465 1078 #edb/ref 17592186142469 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142620 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n![](images/9f/9f7b4224da0b74247411a42ff7786defaa871f790874dc9f7e8b390f769ea65c.svg)" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142620 1071 "What is the missing digit in the following equality?\n\n$0$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142620 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{7}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot \\frac{7}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}$." 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142620 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{7}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times \\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}{7}}.$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142621 1071 "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} ?$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142621 1071 "What is the value of $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$?" 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142621 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{3}{2} \\cdot \\frac{4}{3} &= \\frac{3}{3} \\cdot \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142621 1074 "First, we write $1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 1)+1}{2}= \\dfrac {3} {2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 1{3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 3)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {4}{3}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\times \\frac{4}{3} & =\\frac{3}{3}\\times \\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =1\\times 2 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 1 {2} \\times 1\\,\\dfrac 1 {3} = 2.\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142623 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} = \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142623 1074 "First, we write $3\\,\\dfrac 1{2}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac 1 {2}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{2}= \\dfrac{7}{2}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n1\\,\\dfrac 2 {5}=\\dfrac{(1\\times 5)+2}{5}= \\dfrac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. We multiply the numerators, and we multiply the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 7 {2} \\times \\dfrac{7}{5} =  \\dfrac {7\\times 7} {2\\times 5} = \\dfrac{49}{10}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{49}{10} =4\\,\\textrm{R} 9 = 4\\,\\dfrac 9 {10}.\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142625 1074 "First, we write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}\n$$\n\nas an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers.\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{7}{3} \\cdot \\frac{9}{4} &= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot \\frac{9}{3} \\\\\n&= \\frac{7}{4} \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&= \\frac{21}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 false] [17592186142625 1074 "First, we write $2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {3}=\\dfrac{(3\\times 2)+1}{3}= \\dfrac {7} {3}\n$$\n\nWe also write  $2\\,\\dfrac 1{4}$ as an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n2\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}=\\dfrac{(2\\times 4)+1}{4}= \\dfrac {9}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, we can multiply the two numbers. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify this product by swapping the denominators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{3}\\times \\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times \\frac{9}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\times 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{21}{4} = 5\\,\\textrm{R} 1= 5\\,\\dfrac 1 {4}.\n$$" 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435279 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435281 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435286 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142809 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435288 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435277 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435278 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435285 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435289 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435292 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435282 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435284 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435287 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142811 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435291 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [17592186142812 1038 #edb/ref 17592186435275 13194139534759 17592186045417 true] [175921861